{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"CORRECTION — I was wrong to call a plan-review finding fabricated; it was REAL.\n\nThe reviewer blocked on `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_capability_guard_a3fa.py` unpacking\n`_update_one_dispatch_links` as a 2-tuple. I checked, found no such file, and recorded it as a\nhallucination. My check was run in the MAIN CHECKOUT (/Users/joeoakhart/rebar), which sits at\nan older commit; the file landed on origin/main 2 hours earlier in 671fa77c88 (\"Guard\ncapability call sites at runtime\") and IS present in this worktree's base (5a3ad479f0). Same\nrepo, two different trees — I verified against the wrong one.\n\nIt surfaced exactly as the reviewer predicted, on the full suite:\n\n    tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_capability_guard_a3fa.py:186: ValueError:\n    too many values to unpack (expected 2)\n\nBoth call sites (lines 186 and 264) are now updated to the 3-tuple, and the second one —\n\"a real link failure MUST still feed the silent-no-op canary\" — now also asserts\n`failed == 1`, which is the counter that distinguishes it from the designed capability skip\nasserted at line 186. That distinction is the point of this ticket, so the file is a\nbetter test for the change, not merely a compile fix. Added to file_impact.\n\nLesson recorded for the fleet: \"grep found nothing\" is only evidence about the tree you\ngreped. Verify a plan-review citation against the WORKTREE BASE, not a stale checkout, before\nconcluding a finding is invented. The reviewer was grounded and I was not."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786531975280112001,"uuid":"5ec100e6-6532-4f59-a919-36a754b92c53"}